Linux: import the root pool at boot when built in

With ZFS built into the kernel and no initramfs, nothing imports the pool
before the kernel mounts root= through mount_nodev_root(), so a ZFS root
needs an initramfs on Linux, unlike on FreeBSD. When the kernel command
line has root=<pool>/<dataset> rootfstype=zfs and there is no /init, a
late initcall now assembles the pool config from the vdev labels of the
block devices present, the way spa_generate_rootconf() does on FreeBSD,
points the leaf vdevs at the devices their labels were found on and
imports the pool with cachefile=none. Only devices with a valid L0 or L1
count, since the end of a whole disk can carry the back labels of its
last partition or stale ones. It waits up to
zfs.zfs_boot_import_timeout_ms for the devices of every top-level vdev

diff --git a/module/Kbuild.in b/module/Kbuild.in
